    "proof_tex": "Let \\(\\mathcal E_n=\\{\\phi_n=1\\}\\), where \\(\\phi_n\\) is the split residual-cumulant test in \\(\\textup{def:cumulant-test}\\).  Lemma \\(\\textup{lem:branch-selection-error}\\) and the common supplied-fit condition give, for every law in the Gaussian prior support,\n\\[\n Q_P(\\mathcal E_n)\\le p_{\\mathrm{sel},n},\n\\]\nand, for every law in the non-Gaussian prior support,\n\\[\n Q_P(\\mathcal E_n^c)\\le p_{\\mathrm{sel},n}.\n\\]\nIntegrating the first inequality with respect to \\(\\Pi_{G,n}\\) and the second with respect to \\(\\Pi_{N,n}\\) yields\n\\[\n \\overline Q_{G,n}(\\mathcal E_n)\\le p_{\\mathrm{sel},n},\n \\qquad\n \\overline Q_{N,n}(\\mathcal E_n^c)\\le p_{\\mathrm{sel},n}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor fixed \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(C_{\\mathrm{env}}\\), the explicit median-of-means calibration in \\(\\textup{def:mom-calibration}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\rho_n(\\alpha/16)=O(n^{-1/2}).\n\\]\nDefinition \\(\\textup{def:rate-scales}\\) gives \\(s_n\\ge n^{-1/2}\\).  Therefore \\(\\textup{def:selection-error-envelope}\\) implies, for a constant depending only on the fixed envelope and confidence level,\n\\[\n p_{\\mathrm{sel},n}\n \\le C\\left\\{\n \\left(\\frac{s_n}{\\delta_n}\\right)^2\n + \\left(\\frac{n^{-1/2}}{\\delta_n}\\right)^2\n \\right\\}\n \\le 2C\\left(\\frac{s_n}{\\delta_n}\\right)^2\n =o(1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(0<p_{\\mathrm{sel},n}<1\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  Apply \\(\\textup{lem:mixture-test-chi-square-certificate}\\) to (1), with \\(\\mathsf M_0=\\overline Q_{G,n}\\), \\(\\mathsf M_1=\\overline Q_{N,n}\\), \\(\\psi=\\phi_n\\), and \\(e=p_{\\mathrm{sel},n}\\).  It gives both asserted divergence inequalities.  Equation (2) makes their common lower bound diverge.  The proof did not use the partition size, the form of the nuisance priors, a Hermite expansion, or the target displacement.  It therefore supplies an analytic infeasibility certificate for every finite Rademacher-partition program satisfying the requested support, common-fit, and bounded-chi-square constraints in the strongly separated regime.",
